      "text": "Lecture 2a: Relational Model - Concepts and Terminology\n\n-> Proposed by E.F. Codd\n-> The data is stored in form of Tables.\n-> A Table represents an \"Entity\" (eg: Student). A Table is also called a Relation.\n-> The columns, also called as Attributes, Fields represent a property of the entity (eg: name, class, phone etc.)\n-> The rows, also called records, tuples, data represent one specific entity and the values of it's attributes.\n-> Tuple: Fixed-size, ordered list of values.\n\n-> Domain of an Attribute: The set of atomic values that an attribute can take is called a domain.\n-> Here, \"Atomic\" is used in the context that, the values stored are considered to be indivisible. Not, only indivisible values are stored.\n\n-> Schema of a Table: Represents structure of the table. The table name, attribute names, attribute types and domain of each attribute.\n-> Instance of a Table: Snapshot of a table with real data at a specific point of time.\n\n-> Degree/Arity of a Table: Number of attributes.\n-> Cardinality of a Table: Number of rows in a table at a specific instance.\n\n\n\nLecture 2b: Why a table is called Relation?\n\n-> In Mathematics, a Binary Relation, {(a1, b1), (a2, b2)} is called a relation if ai E A, bi E B and {(a1, b1), (a2, b2)} is subset of A x B\n-> Same way a table is an n-ary relation, since it is a subset of D1 x D2 x . . . x Dn, where Di is domain of each attribute.\n-> Since, a table is a set, the order of rows doesn't matter and no two rows are identical.\n-> But, the same way the order of values in an element of relation matters, the order of values of attributes in a row matters. Hence a row is also called\n    \"Tuple\".\n\n\n\nLecture 2c: The Relational Model - Null Values\n\n-> An attribute can have a \"Null\" as a value. It can be used when data is not applicable, not known, not recorded.\n-> If an attribute shouldn't ever take \"Null\" as a value, it can be specified in schema for specific attributes to not allow \"Null\" as a value.\n\n\n\nLecture 2d: Keys\n\n-> Super Key: A combination or set of attributes, that can identify all the rows in a relation uniquely.\n-> Candidate Key: A Minimal Super Key. Only those attributes that are strictly necessary are included in the key.\n-> Primary Key: One of the Candidate Keys chosen to uniquely indentify each row.\n\n-> In Formal, relational theory, there are no \"NULL\" values and hence introduction of null values in the tables isn't really defined in it.\n-> But in real world, the attributes can be NULLs. And in such cases the definitions of super key and candidate key sway towards ambiguity.\n\n-> Most DBMSs handle them like this. Each comparision of a key, can yeild, TRUE, FALSE and UNKNOWN(In case of when null is present \n    in the same attribute and rest are identical).\n-> If the comparision yields TRUE, the system will reject the entry or modification.\n\n-> So, the course said, Super Keys and Candidate Keys are those keys that can uniquely identify rows that don't have null values in the key attributes.\n-> And Primary Keys, mustn't have null values anyway.\n\n-> The candiate keys that are not chosen to be Primary Keys are called \"Alternate Keys\".\n-> A Candiate Key or Simple key which has only one attribute is called \"Simple Key\", If not they are called \"Composite Keys\".\n\n\n\nLecture 4a: Integrity Constraints\n\n-> Domain Constraint\n-> Not Null Constraint\n-> Key Constraint\n-> Entity Integrity Constraint\n-> Primary Key Constraint\n\n\n\nLecture 4b,c: Referential Integrity - Foriegn Key\n\n-> Foriegn Key: The Column in a table that is a \"Key\" in another/foriegn table. Usually, \"Key\" in foriegn table would be a \"Primary key\".\n-> We say the table with foriegn key is referecning another table. The other table is called \"Referenced Table\" or \"Parent Table\". \"Referecing Table\"\n    is also called \"Child Table\".\n-> The value of a foriegn key in child table must exist in atleast one of the rows of parent table. This constraint is called Referential Integrity.\n\n-> When this is violated, the DBMS can reject the operation that caused the violation or it can do some additional work to enforce it. \n-> Those operations are No Action(Default), Cascade, Set to NULL or Default Value.\n\n-> The constraints and operations are defined at the child table creation.\n\nThere are two scenarois.\n-> A Modification happend in Parent Table.\n    - A new row (there by key) is inserted. This would never cause a violation.\n    - A row (there by key) is deleted. If we set the operation as No Action, The DBMS rejects the deletion. If Cascade, it deltes all rows in child table where \n        the deleted key is present as foriegn key. If Set to NULL or Value, then it will set the value in all rows of child table where the deleted key is referenced\n        to either NULL or the Value mentioned.\n    - The same would happen when the \"key\" is modified.\n\n-> A modification in Child Table.\n    - If it causes a violation, it is rejected.\n\n-> A table can have multiple foriegn keys\n-> A table can have a foriegn key from itself.\n\n\n\nLecture 5a: Functional Dependency\n\n-> Another kind of integrity constraint defined on schema.\n-> Let R be a relation and a, b, c, d, e be attributes. If we define the functional dependency constraint a->b (\"b is functionally dependent on a\" or \"a determines b\") \n    in the schema then for any two tuples t1 and t2, If t1.a = t2.a then t1.b = t2.b\n\n-> Given an instance of schema, r(R), we say this instance satisfies functional dependency a -> b, iff for any two tuples t1 and t2, if t1.a = t2.a then t1.b = t2.b holds.\n-> Given a schema r(R), we say the functional dependency a -> b holds iff every legal instance of r(R) satisfies the functional dependency.\n\n-> Functional Dependencies can be created on multiple attributes. eg: ab -> cd or {a, b} -> {c, d} says that if the combination of values of a and b are same in two tuples,\n    then the combination of values of c and d in those tuples will be same as well.\n\n-> We cannot infer a functional dependency by analysing an instance. We can conclude if a dependency is not satisfied, but we cannot say if a dependency is satisified.\n\nTypes of Dependencies:\n-> Trivial: Let X and Y be sets of attributes and X->Y, we say the dependency is trivial if Y is a subset of X.\n-> Non-Trivial: Let X and Y be sets of attributes and X->Y, we say the dependency is non-trivial if Y is not a subset of X.\n-> Completely Non-Trivial: Let X and Y be sets of attributes and X->Y, we say the dependency is non-trivial if X intersection Y is an empty set.\n\n\n\nLecture 5b: Functional Dependency Laws, Closure of Attribute Set\n\nFunctional Dependency Laws:\n-> Reflexivity (Law of Trivial FDs): If Y is a subset of X, then X->Y.\n-> Augumentation: If X -> Y, then XZ -> YZ\n-> Transitivity: If X -> Y and Y -> Z, then X -> Z\n\nThe above three are called \"Armstrong's Axioms\".\n\n-> Decompositions (Split on RHS): If X -> YZ, then X -> Y and X -> Z\n-> Union (Combine on RHS): If X -> Y and X -> Z, then X -> YZ\n-> Pseudo Transitivity: If X -> Y and WY -> Z, then WX -> Z\n-> Composition: If X -> Y and Z -> W, then XZ -> YW.\n\nAfter studying from lectures and solving questions, we could think about them logically and infer the result like we do in propositional logic.\n\nClosure of Attribute Set:\n-> Given a set of functional dependencies F and a set of attributes X, the closure of X with respect to F,\n    denoted by X+ is the set of all attributes whose values are determined by values of X because of F.\n\n-> If X contains A, B then closure is denoted by AB+ or {A, B}+.\n\n-> Procedue to find closure: Start with the initial attributes. Go through the functional dependencies, add the deduced attributes to initial set. Repeat until \n    you cannot deduce a new attribute.\n\n\nKeys in terms of Functional Dependencies:\n-> If it is given that an attribute in a relation cannot have duplicate values, what would be the closure of such attribute?\n-> Since, there would be only one row in any legal instance of r(R), with a specific value of such attribute, the rest of the attributes are always determined.\n-> So, If A is such attribute, then A+ = R.\n\n-> This is nothing but a KEY.\n-> For Super Key, X+ = R.\n-> For Candidate Key, X+ = R and Candidate Key must be minimal, i.e. Let Y be any PROPER subset of X, then Y+ must not be equal to R.\n    i.e. no proper subset of X is a Super Key.\n\n\n\nLecture 5c: Practice - Finding Candidate Keys\n\n\n\n\n\nLecture 5d: Functional Dependency - Inference and Equivalence\n\n-> Given a set of FDs (S), If an FD (f) can be derived or infered from S, Then we say \"S infers/implies f\" or \"f is infered/implied by S\".\n-> Notation: S |= f or S |- f\n\n-> Closure of a set of FDs(S), S+ is the set of all FDs that can be infered by S.\n-> S+ = { f : S |= f }\n\n-> To find the cardinality of the Closure of S: Start with one attribute on LHS and calculate closure of LHS, Repeat for two, three, four... for all combinations. And Sum.\n\nCovering:\n-> Given two sets of FDs F and G, we say F covers G iff all FDs in G can be derived from F, i.e. F+ is superset of G+. Notation : F => G\n-> We say two sets of FDs F and G are equivalent, iff F covers G and G covers A, i.e. F+ = G+. Notation : F three dashes G\n\n\n\nLecture 5e: Minimal Cover\n\n-> Minimal Cover(Fm)/Canonical Cover(Fc)/Irreducible FD Set(Fir): of a given set of FDs (F) is the irreducible set such that Fm is equivalent to F. i.e. if any functional dependency or attribute is\n    removed from Fm, Fm is no longer equivalent to F.\n\n-> Extraneous attribute: In a given set of FDs (S), an attribute in a functional dependency is said to be extraneous, if the removal of such attribute doesn't change the closure\n    of S. \n-> Given a member of FD set (S), F = X -> Y, to test if one of the attributes in X, say A, is extraneous or not, Find the Closure of (X - A) based on the other members\n    of S, If it cotains Y, Then A is an extraneous attribute.\n-> Redudant FD in a given set of FDs (S), is the FD whose removal doesn't change the closure of S.\n-> To test if an FD X -> Y is redundant or not, Find the closure of X based on the other members of S. If it cotains Y, it is redundant.\n\nProcedure to find Minimal Cover:\n-> Split all the dependencies, so that the RHS shall have only single attribute.\n-> Remove extraneous attributes from the FDs\n-> Remove Redundant FDs\n-> The resultant FDs form a Minimal Cover.\n\n-> Minimal Covers may not be unique.\n-> This because, the order of choosing extraneous attributes and redundant FDs for removal changes the resultant Minimal Cover.\n-> We may choose minimal cover with smallest number of dependencies or the cover with smallest number of total attributes in it.\n\n\n\nLecture 6a: Normal Forms - Partial, Transitive FD\n\n-> Prime Attribute: Attribute which is a part of atleast one candidate key. Non-Prime otherwise.\n\n-> For everything below and further Normal Forms discussion consider that RHS is a single Attribute.\n\n-> Full Functional Dependency: Given X -> A E FD set (S), Then we say A is fully functional dependent on X, iff for all proper subsets of X, Y\n    Y doesn't determine A (Y -> A is False.)\n\n-> Partial Functional Dependency: Given X -> A E FD set (S), Then we say A is paritially functional dependent on X, iff there exists a proper subset of X, Y\n    Y determines A (Y -> A is True)\n\n-> Transitive Functional Dependency: X -> A is transitive FD if there exists Y, such that X -> Y and Y -> A and Y doesn't determine X and A must not be a subset of X or Y.\n\n\n\nLecture 6b,c,d,e: Normal Forms - 1NF, 2NF, 3NF, BCNF\n\n-> 1NF: Domains of all attributes are considered to be atomic\n-> 2NF: \n    - There is no partial dependency from any candidate key to a non-prime attribute (or)\n    - Every non-prime attribute is fully dependent on each candidate key (or)\n    - No non-prime attribute is partially dependent on any candidate key\n    - How to check 2NF? Find all Candidate Keys. Check if some proper subset of some CK determines a Non-Prime Attribute.\n\n-> 3NF:\n    - There is no transitive dependency from any candidate key to a non-prime attribute (or)\n    - There is no full or partial dependency from a non-super key to non-prime attribute\n-> BCNF:\n    - No set of attributes must be determined anything but Super Keys\n\n\n\nLecture 7a,b,c : Normalization, Decomposition, Lossy and Lossless\n\n-> Natural Join - Natural way of joining two tables:\n    1) Find the common attributes with same name\n    2) Equate the values and fill new entries from second table corresponding to the equated value in the second table.\n    3) Take the common attributes column only once in the new table\n\n-> Only those rows in first table whose common attribute values match with one of the values in second table are present in the new table.\n-> If there are no common attributes at all, the join will be a cartesian product of rows of both tables.\n\nRedundancy in Tables:\n- Fact Redundancy: Storing more than one INDEPENDENT attributes in a single table.\n- Data Redundancy: This is a consequence of Fact Redundancy. A number of values of some attributes repeat themselves in the SINGLE table.\n\n-> They cause problems/anamolies: Updation, Insertion and Deletion.\n-> The solution is DECOMPOSITION. i.e. split the SINGLE table into multiple individual tables.\n-> If R is a the original Relation and R1 ... Rn are smaller relations. A Decomposition must satisfy the Attribute preservation property. \n    i.e R1 U R2 U . . . Rn = R\n\n-> Two important properties we need to consider while decomposing:\n\n    1) Lossless-join property: enables us to recover an instance of the original relation by joing instances of smaller decomposed relations without\n      adding any ambiguity.\n    - If it is not satisified, the result of join, would cotain spurious rows which are unwanted and wrong records which were never present in the original\n      relation.\n    - In Binary Decomposition, We make sure this never happens by, choosing common attributes that are a super key in atleast one of the two smaller relations.\n      i.e. (R1 n R2) -> R1 or (R1 n R2) -> R2.\n    - For a lossy Decomposition: R1 join R2 join R3 . . . Rn is a proper superset of R\n    - For a lossless Decomposition: R1 join R2 join R3 . . . Rn is equal to R\n\n    2) Dependency Preservation: enables us to enforce the FDs of original relation onto each of the smaller relations. i.e. we don't need to perform joins on\n        smaller relations to check if an FD of original relation is violated in the smaller tables.\n        - The decomposition of R with a set of FDs F is a set of tables Ri each with a set of FDs Fi containly only the attributes of Ri\n            each Fi is a subset of F+.\n\n    How to check Dependency Preserving Property:\n    -> Find out Fi of every Ri.\n    -> Check if every FD of the original Relation R is covered by (R1 U R2 U ... Rn).\n    -> If yes, then there is no need to join tables for querying or for checking the validity of original FDs in the smaller tables.\n\n\n-> Normalization: is the process of analysing the given relation schemas based on their FDs and Primary keys to acheive the desirable properties of\n    1) minimizing redundancy 2) minimizing the insertion, deletion and update anomalies.\n\n-> It can be considered as a purificaion process to make the design better. It is done by choosing a Normal Form that must be satisfied\n    by the schema and adopting further Normal Forms as needed.\n\n\n\nLecture 7d: Non-Binary Decomposition and Chase Test\n\n-> For non-binary decomposition, the Dependency Preserving property can be checked  same as Binary Decomposition.\n-> But, lossless-join property should be tested via \"Chase Algorithm\".\n\n\n\nLecture 8a: 2NF Decomposition\n\n-> Steps to decompose any relation into 2NF:\n    1) Find out the FDs which are causing violations.\n    2) The new Sub-Relations will be R1( R minus (i = 0 ot n) (closure of LHSi) plus (LHSi) )\n    3) And the Rest will be closures of each of the LHS in the violating FDs\n\n\n\nLecture 8b,c: 3NF Decomposition and BCNF Decomposition\n\n-> BCNF is exactly same as 2NF. This doesn't gurantee \"Dependency Preservation\"\n\n-> Steps to decompose into 3NF (3NF Synthesis):\n    1) Find the Minimal Cover of the FDs of the given relation.\n    2) If LHS is same in any of the FDs in the Minimal Cover, combine them.\n    3) For each FD create a relation with only those attributes.\n    4) If none of the relations contain a Candidate Key, Create a new relation such that attributes are those of any one Candidate Key.\n    5) If there are any new relations which are subset of bigger relations remove them.\n\n\n-> IT IS ALWAYS GUARANTEED THAT DECOMPOSITION TO ANY NORMAL FORM IS \"LOSSLESS\".\n-> IT IS ALWAYS \"POSSIBLE\" TO HAVE 2NF AND 3NF FORMS SATISFY \"DEPENDENCY PRESERVATION'\n-> BCNF MAY OR MAY NOT SATISFY DEPENDENCY PRESERVATION.\n-> THE MAIN OBJECTIVE OF NORMALIZATION IS TO AVOID ANOMALIES AND REDUNDANCY\n\n\nOptional : MVDs",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lecture 2a: Relational Model - Concepts and Terminology\n\n-> Proposed by E.F. Codd\n-> The data is stored in form of Tables.\n-> A Table represents an \"Entity\" (eg: Student). A Table is also called a Relation.\n-> The columns, also called as Attributes, Fields represent a property of the entity (eg: name, class, phone etc.)\n-> The rows, also called records, tuples, data represent one specific entity and the values of it's attributes.\n-> Tuple: Fixed-size, ordered list of values.\n\n-> Domain of an Attribute: The set of atomic values that an attribute can take is called a domain.\n-> Here, \"Atomic\" is used in the context that, the values stored are considered to be indivisible. Not, only indivisible values are stored.\n\n-> Schema of a Table: Represents structure of the table. The table name, attribute names, attribute types and domain of each attribute.\n-> Instance of a Table: Snapshot of a table with real data at a specific point of time.\n\n-> Degree/Arity of a Table: Number of attributes.\n-> Cardinality of a Table: Number of rows in a table at a specific instance.\n\n\n\nLecture 2b: Why a table is called Relation?\n\n-> In Mathematics, a Binary Relation, {(a1, b1), (a2, b2)} is called a relation if ai E A, bi E B and {(a1, b1), (a2, b2)} is subset of A x B\n-> Same way a table is an n-ary relation, since it is a subset of D1 x D2 x . . . x Dn, where Di is domain of each attribute.\n-> Since, a table is a set, the order of rows doesn't matter and no two rows are identical.\n-> But, the same way the order of values in an element of relation matters, the order of values of attributes in a row matters. Hence a row is also called\n    \"Tuple\".\n\n\n\nLecture 2c: The Relational Model - Null Values\n\n-> An attribute can have a \"Null\" as a value. It can be used when data is not applicable, not known, not recorded.\n-> If an attribute shouldn't ever take \"Null\" as a value, it can be specified in schema for specific attributes to not allow \"Null\" as a value.\n\n\n\nLecture 2d: Keys\n\n-> Super Key: A combination or set of attributes, that can identify all the rows in a relation uniquely.\n-> Candidate Key: A Minimal Super Key. Only those attributes that are strictly necessary are included in the key.\n-> Primary Key: One of the Candidate Keys chosen to uniquely indentify each row.\n\n-> In Formal, relational theory, there are no \"NULL\" values and hence introduction of null values in the tables isn't really defined in it.\n-> But in real world, the attributes can be NULLs. And in such cases the definitions of super key and candidate key sway towards ambiguity.\n\n-> Most DBMSs handle them like this. Each comparision of a key, can yeild, TRUE, FALSE and UNKNOWN(In case of when null is present \n    in the same attribute and rest are identical).\n-> If the comparision yields TRUE, the system will reject the entry or modification.\n\n-> So, the course said, Super Keys and Candidate Keys are those keys that can uniquely identify rows that don't have null values in the key attributes.\n-> And Primary Keys, mustn't have null values anyway.\n\n-> The candiate keys that are not chosen to be Primary Keys are called \"Alternate Keys\".\n-> A Candiate Key or Simple key which has only one attribute is called \"Simple Key\", If not they are called \"Composite Keys\".\n\n\n\nLecture 4a: Integrity Constraints\n\n-> Domain Constraint\n-> Not Null Constraint\n-> Key Constraint\n-> Entity Integrity Constraint\n-> Primary Key Constraint\n\n\n\nLecture 4b,c: Referential Integrity - Foriegn Key\n\n-> Foriegn Key: The Column in a table that is a \"Key\" in another/foriegn table. Usually, \"Key\" in foriegn table would be a \"Primary key\".\n-> We say the table with foriegn key is referecning another table. The other table is called \"Referenced Table\" or \"Parent Table\". \"Referecing Table\"\n    is also called \"Child Table\".\n-> The value of a foriegn key in child table must exist in atleast one of the rows of parent table. This constraint is called Referential Integrity.\n\n-> When this is violated, the DBMS can reject the operation that caused the violation or it can do some additional work to enforce it. \n-> Those operations are No Action(Default), Cascade, Set to NULL or Default Value.\n\n-> The constraints and operations are defined at the child table creation.\n\nThere are two scenarois.\n-> A Modification happend in Parent Table.\n    - A new row (there by key) is inserted. This would never cause a violation.\n    - A row (there by key) is deleted. If we set the operation as No Action, The DBMS rejects the deletion. If Cascade, it deltes all rows in child table where \n        the deleted key is present as foriegn key. If Set to NULL or Value, then it will set the value in all rows of child table where the deleted key is referenced\n        to either NULL or the Value mentioned.\n    - The same would happen when the \"key\" is modified.\n\n-> A modification in Child Table.\n    - If it causes a violation, it is rejected.\n\n-> A table can have multiple foriegn keys\n-> A table can have a foriegn key from itself.\n\n\n\nLecture 5a: Functional Dependency\n\n-> Another kind of integrity constraint defined on schema.\n-> Let R be a relation and a, b, c, d, e be attributes. If we define the functional dependency constraint a->b (\"b is functionally dependent on a\" or \"a determines b\") \n    in the schema then for any two tuples t1 and t2, If t1.a = t2.a then t1.b = t2.b\n\n-> Given an instance of schema, r(R), we say this instance satisfies functional dependency a -> b, iff for any two tuples t1 and t2, if t1.a = t2.a then t1.b = t2.b holds.\n-> Given a schema r(R), we say the functional dependency a -> b holds iff every legal instance of r(R) satisfies the functional dependency.\n\n-> Functional Dependencies can be created on multiple attributes. eg: ab -> cd or {a, b} -> {c, d} says that if the combination of values of a and b are same in two tuples,\n    then the combination of values of c and d in those tuples will be same as well.\n\n-> We cannot infer a functional dependency by analysing an instance. We can conclude if a dependency is not satisfied, but we cannot say if a dependency is satisified.\n\nTypes of Dependencies:\n-> Trivial: Let X and Y be sets of attributes and X->Y, we say the dependency is trivial if Y is a subset of X.\n-> Non-Trivial: Let X and Y be sets of attributes and X->Y, we say the dependency is non-trivial if Y is not a subset of X.\n-> Completely Non-Trivial: Let X and Y be sets of attributes and X->Y, we say the dependency is non-trivial if X intersection Y is an empty set.\n\n\n\nLecture 5b: Functional Dependency Laws, Closure of Attribute Set\n\nFunctional Dependency Laws:\n-> Reflexivity (Law of Trivial FDs): If Y is a subset of X, then X->Y.\n-> Augumentation: If X -> Y, then XZ -> YZ\n-> Transitivity: If X -> Y and Y -> Z, then X -> Z\n\nThe above three are called \"Armstrong's Axioms\".\n\n-> Decompositions (Split on RHS): If X -> YZ, then X -> Y and X -> Z\n-> Union (Combine on RHS): If X -> Y and X -> Z, then X -> YZ\n-> Pseudo Transitivity: If X -> Y and WY -> Z, then WX -> Z\n-> Composition: If X -> Y and Z -> W, then XZ -> YW.\n\nAfter studying from lectures and solving questions, we could think about them logically and infer the result like we do in propositional logic.\n\nClosure of Attribute Set:\n-> Given a set of functional dependencies F and a set of attributes X, the closure of X with respect to F,\n    denoted by X+ is the set of all attributes whose values are determined by values of X because of F.\n\n-> If X contains A, B then closure is denoted by AB+ or {A, B}+.\n\n-> Procedue to find closure: Start with the initial attributes. Go through the functional dependencies, add the deduced attributes to initial set. Repeat until \n    you cannot deduce a new attribute.\n\n\nKeys in terms of Functional Dependencies:\n-> If it is given that an attribute in a relation cannot have duplicate values, what would be the closure of such attribute?\n-> Since, there would be only one row in any legal instance of r(R), with a specific value of such attribute, the rest of the attributes are always determined.\n-> So, If A is such attribute, then A+ = R.\n\n-> This is nothing but a KEY.\n-> For Super Key, X+ = R.\n-> For Candidate Key, X+ = R and Candidate Key must be minimal, i.e. Let Y be any PROPER subset of X, then Y+ must not be equal to R.\n    i.e. no proper subset of X is a Super Key.\n\n\n\nLecture 5c: Practice - Finding Candidate Keys\n\n\n\n\n\nLecture 5d: Functional Dependency - Inference and Equivalence\n\n-> Given a set of FDs (S), If an FD (f) can be derived or infered from S, Then we say \"S infers/implies f\" or \"f is infered/implied by S\".\n-> Notation: S |= f or S |- f\n\n-> Closure of a set of FDs(S), S+ is the set of all FDs that can be infered by S.\n-> S+ = { f : S |= f }\n\n-> To find the cardinality of the Closure of S: Start with one attribute on LHS and calculate closure of LHS, Repeat for two, three, four... for all combinations. And Sum.\n\nCovering:\n-> Given two sets of FDs F and G, we say F covers G iff all FDs in G can be derived from F, i.e. F+ is superset of G+. Notation : F => G\n-> We say two sets of FDs F and G are equivalent, iff F covers G and G covers A, i.e. F+ = G+. Notation : F three dashes G\n\n\n\nLecture 5e: Minimal Cover\n\n-> Minimal Cover(Fm)/Canonical Cover(Fc)/Irreducible FD Set(Fir): of a given set of FDs (F) is the irreducible set such that Fm is equivalent to F. i.e. if any functional dependency or attribute is\n    removed from Fm, Fm is no longer equivalent to F.\n\n-> Extraneous attribute: In a given set of FDs (S), an attribute in a functional dependency is said to be extraneous, if the removal of such attribute doesn't change the closure\n    of S. \n-> Given a member of FD set (S), F = X -> Y, to test if one of the attributes in X, say A, is extraneous or not, Find the Closure of (X - A) based on the other members\n    of S, If it cotains Y, Then A is an extraneous attribute.\n-> Redudant FD in a given set of FDs (S), is the FD whose removal doesn't change the closure of S.\n-> To test if an FD X -> Y is redundant or not, Find the closure of X based on the other members of S. If it cotains Y, it is redundant.\n\nProcedure to find Minimal Cover:\n-> Split all the dependencies, so that the RHS shall have only single attribute.\n-> Remove extraneous attributes from the FDs\n-> Remove Redundant FDs\n-> The resultant FDs form a Minimal Cover.\n\n-> Minimal Covers may not be unique.\n-> This because, the order of choosing extraneous attributes and redundant FDs for removal changes the resultant Minimal Cover.\n-> We may choose minimal cover with smallest number of dependencies or the cover with smallest number of total attributes in it.\n\n\n\nLecture 6a: Normal Forms - Partial, Transitive FD\n\n-> Prime Attribute: Attribute which is a part of atleast one candidate key. Non-Prime otherwise.\n\n-> For everything below and further Normal Forms discussion consider that RHS is a single Attribute.\n\n-> Full Functional Dependency: Given X -> A E FD set (S), Then we say A is fully functional dependent on X, iff for all proper subsets of X, Y\n    Y doesn't determine A (Y -> A is False.)\n\n-> Partial Functional Dependency: Given X -> A E FD set (S), Then we say A is paritially functional dependent on X, iff there exists a proper subset of X, Y\n    Y determines A (Y -> A is True)\n\n-> Transitive Functional Dependency: X -> A is transitive FD if there exists Y, such that X -> Y and Y -> A and Y doesn't determine X and A must not be a subset of X or Y.\n\n\n\nLecture 6b,c,d,e: Normal Forms - 1NF, 2NF, 3NF, BCNF\n\n-> 1NF: Domains of all attributes are considered to be atomic\n-> 2NF: \n    - There is no partial dependency from any candidate key to a non-prime attribute (or)\n    - Every non-prime attribute is fully dependent on each candidate key (or)\n    - No non-prime attribute is partially dependent on any candidate key\n    - How to check 2NF? Find all Candidate Keys. Check if some proper subset of some CK determines a Non-Prime Attribute.\n\n-> 3NF:\n    - There is no transitive dependency from any candidate key to a non-prime attribute (or)\n    - There is no full or partial dependency from a non-super key to non-prime attribute\n-> BCNF:\n    - No set of attributes must be determined anything but Super Keys\n\n\n\nLecture 7a,b,c : Normalization, Decomposition, Lossy and Lossless\n\n-> Natural Join - Natural way of joining two tables:\n    1) Find the common attributes with same name\n    2) Equate the values and fill new entries from second table corresponding to the equated value in the second table.\n    3) Take the common attributes column only once in the new table\n\n-> Only those rows in first table whose common attribute values match with one of the values in second table are present in the new table.\n-> If there are no common attributes at all, the join will be a cartesian product of rows of both tables.\n\nRedundancy in Tables:\n- Fact Redundancy: Storing more than one INDEPENDENT attributes in a single table.\n- Data Redundancy: This is a consequence of Fact Redundancy. A number of values of some attributes repeat themselves in the SINGLE table.\n\n-> They cause problems/anamolies: Updation, Insertion and Deletion.\n-> The solution is DECOMPOSITION. i.e. split the SINGLE table into multiple individual tables.\n-> If R is a the original Relation and R1 ... Rn are smaller relations. A Decomposition must satisfy the Attribute preservation property. \n    i.e R1 U R2 U . . . Rn = R\n\n-> Two important properties we need to consider while decomposing:\n\n    1) Lossless-join property: enables us to recover an instance of the original relation by joing instances of smaller decomposed relations without\n      adding any ambiguity.\n    - If it is not satisified, the result of join, would cotain spurious rows which are unwanted and wrong records which were never present in the original\n      relation.\n    - In Binary Decomposition, We make sure this never happens by, choosing common attributes that are a super key in atleast one of the two smaller relations.\n      i.e. (R1 n R2) -> R1 or (R1 n R2) -> R2.\n    - For a lossy Decomposition: R1 join R2 join R3 . . . Rn is a proper superset of R\n    - For a lossless Decomposition: R1 join R2 join R3 . . . Rn is equal to R\n\n    2) Dependency Preservation: enables us to enforce the FDs of original relation onto each of the smaller relations. i.e. we don't need to perform joins on\n        smaller relations to check if an FD of original relation is violated in the smaller tables.\n        - The decomposition of R with a set of FDs F is a set of tables Ri each with a set of FDs Fi containly only the attributes of Ri\n            each Fi is a subset of F+.\n\n    How to check Dependency Preserving Property:\n    -> Find out Fi of every Ri.\n    -> Check if every FD of the original Relation R is covered by (R1 U R2 U ... Rn).\n    -> If yes, then there is no need to join tables for querying or for checking the validity of original FDs in the smaller tables.\n\n\n-> Normalization: is the process of analysing the given relation schemas based on their FDs and Primary keys to acheive the desirable properties of\n    1) minimizing redundancy 2) minimizing the insertion, deletion and update anomalies.\n\n-> It can be considered as a purificaion process to make the design better. It is done by choosing a Normal Form that must be satisfied\n    by the schema and adopting further Normal Forms as needed.\n\n\n\nLecture 7d: Non-Binary Decomposition and Chase Test\n\n-> For non-binary decomposition, the Dependency Preserving property can be checked  same as Binary Decomposition.\n-> But, lossless-join property should be tested via \"Chase Algorithm\".\n\n\n\nLecture 8a: 2NF Decomposition\n\n-> Steps to decompose any relation into 2NF:\n    1) Find out the FDs which are causing violations.\n    2) The new Sub-Relations will be R1( R minus (i = 0 ot n) (closure of LHSi) plus (LHSi) )\n    3) And the Rest will be closures of each of the LHS in the violating FDs\n\n\n\nLecture 8b,c: 3NF Decomposition and BCNF Decomposition\n\n-> BCNF is exactly same as 2NF. This doesn't gurantee \"Dependency Preservation\"\n\n-> Steps to decompose into 3NF (3NF Synthesis):\n    1) Find the Minimal Cover of the FDs of the given relation.\n    2) If LHS is same in any of the FDs in the Minimal Cover, combine them.\n    3) For each FD create a relation with only those attributes.\n    4) If none of the relations contain a Candidate Key, Create a new relation such that attributes are those of any one Candidate Key.\n    5) If there are any new relations which are subset of bigger relations remove them.\n\n\n-> IT IS ALWAYS GUARANTEED THAT DECOMPOSITION TO ANY NORMAL FORM IS \"LOSSLESS\".\n-> IT IS ALWAYS \"POSSIBLE\" TO HAVE 2NF AND 3NF FORMS SATISFY \"DEPENDENCY PRESERVATION'\n-> BCNF MAY OR MAY NOT SATISFY DEPENDENCY PRESERVATION.\n-> THE MAIN OBJECTIVE OF NORMALIZATION IS TO AVOID ANOMALIES AND REDUNDANCY\n\n\nOptional : MVDs",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
