      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Module 2: Relational Algebra\n\nLecture 1, 2: Introduction and Basice Operators\n\n-> Query languages are special languages for querying(Retrieval of data/ Modify/ Insert/ Delete) databases. \n-> Two formal languages were given by codd: Relational Algebra and Relational Calculus. These are theoretical languages from which sql is derived.\n\n-> There are two types of languages: Procedural and Declarative\n-> Procedural languages require user to specify what to do and HOW to do. i.e. user must describe every step of the operation that must be performed.\n-> Declarative languages require the user to only what to do. He can just describe what he wants and he will get the result.\n\n-> For querying databases, the procedural language is relational algebra and the declarative language is relational calculus. \n-> The DBMS software internally uses relational algebra for optimizing SQL queries, which are basically a form of declarative language like relational calculus.\n\n-> In any algebra we are operands and operators. In Number algebra the operands can be integers, floats etc and operators can be +,- etc.\n-> Same way, in relational algebra, operands are instances of relations and there are specific operators. and the result of an operations is also an instance.\n\n\nBasic/Primitive Operators in Relational Algebra:\n\n1) Selection:\n    -> Selction operator is an unary opeartor which takes an instance of a relation and filters out tuples based on the condition given.\n    -> σ_condition (R)\n    -> R is the instance of the relation and condition is a \"propositional predicate\". the predicate can have multiple terms connected by propositional AND, OR and NEGATION.\n    -> Each term is of type: \"<attribute> op <attribute>\" or \"<attribute> op constant\". where op can be any relational operator.\n    -> It doesn't change the schema of the input relation, only filters out tuples. so, cardinality of the result instance might change.\n    -> The condition is applied individually on each tuple. So it is impossible to write conditions that specifies any notation to involve more than one tuple. eg: Quantificaion Operators\n\n2) Projection:\n    -> Projection operator filters columns in a given instance. This is an unary operator.\n    -> It only outputs those attributes which are mentioned in the notation.\n    -> It might be possible to have lesser cardinality in the result relation than in the original relation. Becoz, the instance itself is a RELATION which doesn't allow duplicate entries.\n    -> If the attributes mentioned form a key, then the cardinality won't change. \n    -> π_attribute_list (R)      π_name, email (Users)\n\nProperties of Selection and Projection:\n-> Selection operation is commutative.\n-> Projection is not.\n-> When Projection is involved and you want to check the validity of the given expression, always remember to follow the order of evaluation and the attributes\n    mentioned. because when two projection operations are chained together, the outer most operator can work only if it's attributes are a subset of the inner most operator attributes.\n    In which case, we'd be better off removing the inner operator.\n\n3) Union 4) Difference:\n    -> These are binary operators. The symbols are same as set notations.\n    -> For these the schema of the two relations must match. i.e. the number of attributes must be same and their domains must be same. Don't care about the names.\n    -> Intersection is a derived attribute of these. So, it is not considered a \"Primitive\" operator.\n    -> It is said that the instances must be \"Union-Compatible\" to have them operate with the above three operators.\n\n5) Cartesian Product or Cross Product:\n    -> This is also a binary operator. RxS\n    -> Every tuple in R is paired with every tuple in S. The resultant instance will have |R| x |S| tuples in it.\n\n-> For Union, Difference, Intersectin and Cross Product, we don't care about the order of attributes in the resultant instance. We only care about the actual data in the tuples.\n    So, they are considered to satisfy Commutative Property.\n\n6) Rename Operator:\n    -> Used to rename the relation name and or the attribute names.\n    -> ρ_NewRelationName(new_column1, new_column2) (R)\n\n-> The original relations that we give as input are never changed by relational algebra. Rather, a new temporary instance of the original instance is created and the \n    operations are perfomred on this and the result is produced.\n\n\nLecture 3, 4: Join Operations and Divison Operation\n\n-> For some cases to retrive data, we might need to combine two or more tables.\n-> Although Cross Product combines, it gives a huge table with all possible combinations which is unnecessary.\n-> For more efficient combining we have Join Operators.\n\n(Inner) JOIN Operators:\n1) Conditional Join or Theta-Join:\n    -> It is basically, filtering out tuples from the cross product. But there are efficient JOIN ALGORITHMS that skip the cross-product but acheive the final result.\n    -> R ⋈_θ S   or  R ⋈_condition S\n    -> Only those tuples from cross-product which are a match for the condition are present in the result. The left out tuples from R and S are called \"Dangling tuples\" or\n        \"Dangling Rows\".\n\n2) Equi-Join:\n    -> This is nothing but a conditional join, but the condition contains only \"equality operator(=)\". \n    -> R ⋈_(A=B) S\n\n3) Natural Join:\n    -> This the natural way of joining that we have seen earlier. R ⋈ S.\n    -> It takes the common attributes in both the relations. and Pairs tuples from R and S, which have the same value for the common attributes.\n    -> There is no way to specify the attributes that the user want to match. it just takes the whole set of common attributes as a tuple as the matching value.\n    -> If there are no common attributes, then the result is a cross-product.\n\n\nDivision Operation:\n-> R ÷ S\n-> In basic math division, Dividend/Divisor = Quotient. We can interpret it as, \"Quotient is the largest value when multipled by Divisor gives a value less than or equal to Dividend\".\n-> In a Relation Prespective, R / S = Q, can be interpreted as, \"Q is the largest relation when cross producted with S give a result which is a SUBSET of R\"\n-> So, If R(A, B) and S(B), then R ÷ S, gives Q(A), such that R contains every tuple of S x Q.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Module 2: Relational Algebra\n\nLecture 1, 2: Introduction and Basice Operators\n\n-> Query languages are special languages for querying(Retrieval of data/ Modify/ Insert/ Delete) databases. \n-> Two formal languages were given by codd: Relational Algebra and Relational Calculus. These are theoretical languages from which sql is derived.\n\n-> There are two types of languages: Procedural and Declarative\n-> Procedural languages require user to specify what to do and HOW to do. i.e. user must describe every step of the operation that must be performed.\n-> Declarative languages require the user to only what to do. He can just describe what he wants and he will get the result.\n\n-> For querying databases, the procedural language is relational algebra and the declarative language is relational calculus. \n-> The DBMS software internally uses relational algebra for optimizing SQL queries, which are basically a form of declarative language like relational calculus.\n\n-> In any algebra we are operands and operators. In Number algebra the operands can be integers, floats etc and operators can be +,- etc.\n-> Same way, in relational algebra, operands are instances of relations and there are specific operators. and the result of an operations is also an instance.\n\n\nBasic/Primitive Operators in Relational Algebra:\n\n1) Selection:\n    -> Selction operator is an unary opeartor which takes an instance of a relation and filters out tuples based on the condition given.\n    -> σ_condition (R)\n    -> R is the instance of the relation and condition is a \"propositional predicate\". the predicate can have multiple terms connected by propositional AND, OR and NEGATION.\n    -> Each term is of type: \"<attribute> op <attribute>\" or \"<attribute> op constant\". where op can be any relational operator.\n    -> It doesn't change the schema of the input relation, only filters out tuples. so, cardinality of the result instance might change.\n    -> The condition is applied individually on each tuple. So it is impossible to write conditions that specifies any notation to involve more than one tuple. eg: Quantificaion Operators\n\n2) Projection:\n    -> Projection operator filters columns in a given instance. This is an unary operator.\n    -> It only outputs those attributes which are mentioned in the notation.\n    -> It might be possible to have lesser cardinality in the result relation than in the original relation. Becoz, the instance itself is a RELATION which doesn't allow duplicate entries.\n    -> If the attributes mentioned form a key, then the cardinality won't change. \n    -> π_attribute_list (R)      π_name, email (Users)\n\nProperties of Selection and Projection:\n-> Selection operation is commutative.\n-> Projection is not.\n-> When Projection is involved and you want to check the validity of the given expression, always remember to follow the order of evaluation and the attributes\n    mentioned. because when two projection operations are chained together, the outer most operator can work only if it's attributes are a subset of the inner most operator attributes.\n    In which case, we'd be better off removing the inner operator.\n\n3) Union 4) Difference:\n    -> These are binary operators. The symbols are same as set notations.\n    -> For these the schema of the two relations must match. i.e. the number of attributes must be same and their domains must be same. Don't care about the names.\n    -> Intersection is a derived attribute of these. So, it is not considered a \"Primitive\" operator.\n    -> It is said that the instances must be \"Union-Compatible\" to have them operate with the above three operators.\n\n5) Cartesian Product or Cross Product:\n    -> This is also a binary operator. RxS\n    -> Every tuple in R is paired with every tuple in S. The resultant instance will have |R| x |S| tuples in it.\n\n-> For Union, Difference, Intersectin and Cross Product, we don't care about the order of attributes in the resultant instance. We only care about the actual data in the tuples.\n    So, they are considered to satisfy Commutative Property.\n\n6) Rename Operator:\n    -> Used to rename the relation name and or the attribute names.\n    -> ρ_NewRelationName(new_column1, new_column2) (R)\n\n-> The original relations that we give as input are never changed by relational algebra. Rather, a new temporary instance of the original instance is created and the \n    operations are perfomred on this and the result is produced.\n\n\nLecture 3, 4: Join Operations and Divison Operation\n\n-> For some cases to retrive data, we might need to combine two or more tables.\n-> Although Cross Product combines, it gives a huge table with all possible combinations which is unnecessary.\n-> For more efficient combining we have Join Operators.\n\n(Inner) JOIN Operators:\n1) Conditional Join or Theta-Join:\n    -> It is basically, filtering out tuples from the cross product. But there are efficient JOIN ALGORITHMS that skip the cross-product but acheive the final result.\n    -> R ⋈_θ S   or  R ⋈_condition S\n    -> Only those tuples from cross-product which are a match for the condition are present in the result. The left out tuples from R and S are called \"Dangling tuples\" or\n        \"Dangling Rows\".\n\n2) Equi-Join:\n    -> This is nothing but a conditional join, but the condition contains only \"equality operator(=)\". \n    -> R ⋈_(A=B) S\n\n3) Natural Join:\n    -> This the natural way of joining that we have seen earlier. R ⋈ S.\n    -> It takes the common attributes in both the relations. and Pairs tuples from R and S, which have the same value for the common attributes.\n    -> There is no way to specify the attributes that the user want to match. it just takes the whole set of common attributes as a tuple as the matching value.\n    -> If there are no common attributes, then the result is a cross-product.\n\n\nDivision Operation:\n-> R ÷ S\n-> In basic math division, Dividend/Divisor = Quotient. We can interpret it as, \"Quotient is the largest value when multipled by Divisor gives a value less than or equal to Dividend\".\n-> In a Relation Prespective, R / S = Q, can be interpreted as, \"Q is the largest relation when cross producted with S give a result which is a SUBSET of R\"\n-> So, If R(A, B) and S(B), then R ÷ S, gives Q(A), such that R contains every tuple of S x Q.\n",
