      "originalText": "Module 6: File Organization and Indexing\n\nFile Organization:\n\n\n\n\nIndexing:\n\n-> Indices are structures that are used for reducing the time taken for random accesses of the records.\n-> Each entry will have <value of the search key, pointer to the block or record>\n-> search key is the attribute or set of attributes choosen for the ordering of entries in the index.\n-> If there is an entry in the index for each record of the table, then it is called a \"Dense Index\". Otherwise \"Sparse Index\".\n-> Index file is always ordered (by search key). But Data file may be unordered, In which case it is called \"Heap File\".\n\nINDEX -\n    Ordered Indices (Static Indices) -\n        Single Level\n        Multi Level\n    \n    Tree Based Indices (Dynamic Indices) -\n        B Tree\n        B+ Tree\n\nSingle Level Indices:\n1) Primary Indices:\n    - The data file must be sorted by a candidate key. Also called (Ordering Key Field)\n    - The same key must be used for index entries as well.\n    - We usually store one entry per block of the data file. i.e. the search key value at the top entry of a block is used as index entry. And since, \n      the physical data in data file is ordered. we can extract the record we want, after the block is read into the main memory.\n    - So, number of entries in Index file is equal to number of blocks in data file.\n    - Number of blocks in Index are significantly lower than number of blocks in data file, coz index entries are very small, <search key value, pointer>\n    - Number of block accesses = ceil(log I) + 1. \n2) Clustering Indices:\n    - The data file must be sorted by a field which is not a KEY. The same key must be used for index entries as well.\n    - We usually store one entry per distinct value of the field chosen. i.e. the block address of the first record which has the same value as the index entry search key value.\n      And the records that have same value shall be stored continuously from it.\n    - So, number of entries in Index file is equal to number of distinct values in the field chosen.\n3) Secondary Indices:\n    - Index on any field or key that is not chosen for ordering of the data file\n    - You can have multiple secondary indices on a table at the same time.\n    - secondary index on a key: Dense with record pointers (or block pointers). This is because the data file is not ordered based on the searcy key. And in worst case, each of it\n      can be present in distinct blocks.\n    - secondary index on a non-key (Implementations: can have variable length entries or repeating index values or a new level to store all record pointers with same search key value).\n\n-> At a time, We can have only one of Primary Index and Clustering Index.\n-> At a time, We cah have only one Primary Index or only one Clustering Index.\n-> At a time we can have Primary Index and Many Secondary indices or Clustering Index and Many Secondary Indices.\n\n\n\nMulti-level Indices:\n\n-> Multi-level indices create PRIMARY INDICES on the first level of Index.\n-> The first level of index can be primary index or clustering index or secondary index.\n-> The outer most layer or Top layer or the layer with highest number shall generally have only one block.\n-> Total number of block accesses will be number of levels + 1 (for the actual data)\n-> ISAM is nothing but a Multilevel Index with Primary or Clustering first level Index.\n\n\n\nB Tree:\n\n-> Dynamic Multi-Level Tree Based Index.\n-> Definition rules of a B Tree of order \"p\".\n    1) Each Node in B Tree is of the following form.\n       <P1, <K1, Pr1>, P2, <K2, Pr2>, P3, <K3, Pr3>, . . . , Pq-1, <Kq-1, Prq-1>, Pq>\n\n       where, q <= p\n       Pi is a \"Tree Pointer\" - A pointer to another node in the tree.\n       Pri is a \"Data Pointer\" - A pointer to the record whose search key value is Ki.\n\n    2) With in each node K1 < K2 < K3 < . . . < Kq-1\n\n    3) For all search key field values X in the subtree pointed by Pi, \n        Ki-1 < X < Ki;   for 1 < i < q\n        X < Ki;           for i = 1\n        X > ki-1;         for i = q \n    \n    4) Each node has atmost p \"Tree Pointers\".\n    5) Each node except root node has atleast ceil(p/2) Tree pointers. Root node must have atleast two Tree pointers unless it is the only node present in the tree.\n    6) A node with q <= p Tree pointers must have q-1 Search Key values and hence q-1 data pointers.\n    7) All leaf nodes are at the same level and have same structure as other nodes except all tree pointers are null in leaf nodes.\n\n\nSearching in B Tree:\n\n-> Search for the key in root node.\n-> if it is not found, explore recursively in the corresponding Tree Pointers.\n\n-> With in each node, it takes O(log p) to search.\n-> So, Total it takes O(log p * height of tree).\n-> Height of Tree in worst case is O(log base p/2 (n)), where n is the \"Total Keys in B Tree\".\n-> O(log base 2 (p) * log base p/2 (n)) which simplifies to O(log base 2 (n))\n\n\nInsertion in B Tree:\n\n-> Insertion always happens at an appropriate leaf node.\n-> eg: consider this as a leaf node and we are inserting a new Key, 16:\n    \n    P1 | 12 | P2 | 20 | P3\n\n    After Insertion:\n    \n    P1 | 12 | P2 | 16 | P3 | 20 | P4\n\n-> Since this is a leaf node, all Pi are null. So, all we need to do is just insert the key at correct place.\n\n-> But, WHAT IF, the leaf node already has \"p\" tree pointers. i.e. the number of tree pointers is equal to order of the tree.\n    in such case, we cannot insert a new key in the same node. This is when we \"SPLIT\" the node.\n\n-> Now, we can have two cases. \n   Case 1: The parent node of the leaf node isn't full.\n\n       In such case, we find the middle key of the leaf node after insertion. and we promote it to the parent.\n       This promotion causes to split the pointer P in the parent node into two pointers. and Hence, the leaf node into two nodes.\n        \n       If the above node is such an example, then the two leaf nodes after splitting are:\n       Before splitting:\n\n       P1 12 P2 20 P3\n\n       After Splitting:\n\n       P1 12 P2 => node 1\n       P3 20 P4 => node 2\n\n       Parent node will be like:\n       Before Splitting: \n       . . . 5 P 30 . . . \n\n       P points to the old leaf node.\n\n       After Splitting:\n       . . . 5 P1 16 P2 30 . . . \n\n       P1 points to node 1 and P2 points to node 2.\n\n\n    Case 2: The parent node of the leaf is full.\n        \n        In such case, we still split the leaf node. And since, the parent node is now overflowing, we try to split the parent node.\n        We do this recursively, until some ancestor in the path is not full. \n        If we reach the root in this process and the root is also full, we split the root and form a new root above it containing the key that is promoted.\n        This results in increment of the HEIGHT of the tree.\n\n\n-> Insertion in a B Tree of even order: In such case, the number of keys will be odd. There can be two ways of splitting at EACH node:\n    left-bias: the left subtree will have an extra node than the right.\n    right-bias: the right subtree will have an extra node than the left.\n\n-> Every non-leaf node in a B Tree will have all of it's Tree pointers as non-null.\n-> The immediate predecessor or inorder predecessor of every key in a non-leaf node resides in a DISTINCT leaf node. Same is true for immediate successor or inorder successor.\n-> Same cannot be said for keys in leaf nodes that pre/suc ALWAYS reside in non-leaf nodes. eg: when mjltiple keys exist in the same leaf node.\n-> Based on this property: Number of leaf nodes in a B Tree = 1 + number of keys in non-leaf nodes.\n\n-> Given Block Size B, order p -> p * (Tree Pointer Size) + (p-1) * (Record Pointer Size + Key Size) <= B\n\n\nMaximum and Minimum number of keys that can exisit in a B Tree, Given the order and height of the tree:\n\nMaximum and Minimum height possible for a B Tree, Given the order and number of keys present in the tree:\n\n\n\nB+ Tree:\n\n-> In B+ Tree, all keys with record pointers are on the leaves unlike B Tree. Though some keys may appear in non-leaf nodes, they are just there for the purpose of searching and the \n    record pointers associated with each key are present only on the leaf nodes.\n-> And in each Leaf, there is only one \"Block Pointer\" That points to the next leaf, the leaf whose first key is just greater than the last key in the current leaf.\n-> Which means, the keys are stored in a sorted order, from the left most leaf to right most leaf.\n\n\nOrder of B+ Tree\n\n\nEquality Search and Range Search in B+ Tree:\n\n\nInsertion in B+ Tree\n\n\nNumber of Keys given the order in B Tree and B+ Tree(Leaf and Internal Nodes):\n\n\nMaximum and Minimum number of keys that can exisit in a B Tree, Given the order and height of the B+ tree:\n\nMaximum and Minimum height possible for a B Tree, Given the order and number of keys present in the B+ tree:",
