      "text": "Module 7: Transaction Management and Recovery\n\n\nTransaction, Read/Write,Database Consistency\n\n-> A Transaction intuitively is a Single Logical Task. But in the POV of DBMS, it is a unit of program execution that involves sequence of Read, Write and some processing operations on data.\n-> Element or Data-item: Is anything in the Database that can be uniquely named or identified. eg: a record, a relation, a paritcular value of attribute in a record, a block etc.\n-> Database State: Is nothing but DB Snapshot or a DB Instance at a certain point of time.\n-> Consistent Database State is when all the explicit constraints(Schema Constraints) and implicit Constraints(Logical Constraints given by the programmer) are satisfied.\n-> Simplified Read Operation: Read(X) -> Reads the block containing X into the memory buffer of the transaction that initiated the read and stores the value of X onto a variable X on MM.\n-> Simplified Write Operation: Write(X) -> Reads the block containing X into the memory buffer of the transaction that initiated the write and stoers the value of X ONLY IF the write requires \n    the old value of X for updating. And writes the new value to X in the MM and stores the block immediately in the disk.\n\n-> For above simplified reads and writes, we made these assumptions which are not correct for actual reads and writes:\n    1) Each transaction has it's own buffer. In reality all transaction share the same buffer.\n    2) Writes store the data immediately on disk. In reality block is written to the disk after the transaction is commited(either right after or after some time has passed).\n\n-> But these assumptions doesn't make our analysis of transactions incorrect. They produce same results as the actual events.\n-> Transactions are COMMITED after they are completed.\n-> If some error occurs in the middle of transaction at any step, then it is ABORTED OR ROLLEDBACK.\n\n-> Executing multiple transactions concurrently can cause inconsistencies and wrong results to occur.\n\n\nACID Properties:\n\n-> These are some properties that must be satisfied by both programmer and DBMS for correct results.\n-> Atomicity: Treat a sequence of operations as one single unit. If all of them are not completed then rollback previous operations.\n    Basically, treats each transaction as \"All or Nothing\"\n-> Consistency: This is not talking about data base consistency. All acid properties help in achieving DB consistency. \n    This particular property in context of ACID is telling that programmer should write correct transactions for the fact that there is no way\n    to deduce the correctness and consistency by DBMS as this is purely logical and depedent on programmer.\n    So, when we say a DB is consistent, we are assuming that this property is satisfied.\n-> Isolation: Provide an illusion to transactions that it is the only thing executing at a time, even though multiple transactions are executhing concurrently.\n-> Durability: If a transaction is commited. The changes must reflect on the disk no matter what happens.\n\n\nSerializability:\n\n-> Schedule: When transactions are executing concurrently in a interleaved fashion, then the order of execution of\n    operations from multiple transactions is known as a Schedule.\n-> If the operations are not interleaved, then it is called \"SERIAL SCHEDULE\". They always preserve consistency.\n\n-> Providing Isolation to transactions that are running concurrently is a very important task.\n-> To do this, DBMS executes transactions using a serializable schedule, the schedule that gurantess to produce same effects as a serial schedule.\n-> A schedule is \"serializable\" iff it produces same effect on database state as SOME serial schedule does on all possible input db states.\n\n-> However, It is important to note that the state of the DB after all such serial schedules is not same. \n-> That is, the order T1, T3, T4, T2 and T2, T3, T1, T4 may produce different state but the state is guranteed to be consistent.\n\n-> This is the definition of serializability in general.\n-> This definition is very broad in the sense that, to detect if a schedule is serializable, the operations that we perform on the data will play a huge role in determining\n    serializability. Ans since we cannot predict the kind of operations that a query might perform on data, it is very difficult to implement this definition.\n\n-> So, we ignore the \"computations\" on data and only focus on \"read/write\" operations in the schedule. And thus, see if it is \"Conflict Serializable\".\n\n-> Conflict Pairs: We say that two operations I and J in a schedule are said to be in confict iff:\n    from different transactions AND on same data item AND atleast one of them is a write.\n-> Swapping two consecutive non-conflicting operations of different transactions doesn't affect the result of schedule.\n\n-> Conflict Equivalent Schedules: If a Schedule S can be transformed into a schedule S' by a series of swaps of Non-Conflicting Consecutive operations of different\n    transactions, we say that S and S' are CONFLIT EQUIVALENT.\n-> i.e. two schedules are conflict equivalent if they can be turned one into other by a sequence of swaps of adjacent/consecutive non-conflicting operations.\n\n-> It is important to note that, we cannot swap non-consecutive operations coz, it might result in swapping of operations from same transaction.\n\n-> AND FINALLY, A schedule is said to be CONFLICT SERIALIZABLE, if it is conflict equivalent to some serial schedule.\n-> Conflict equivalence is transitive.\n\n-> In a schedule, while determining serializability, ignore COMMIT, it's position doesn't affect anything.\n-> But, if you encounter an ABORT, no matter the position, igonre the whole transaction in which it appears.\n\n\n-> Now, given a schedule, i.e. the order of execution of operations from different transactions, how do you find if it is \"CONFLICT SERIALIZABLE\"?\n-> We use something called \"Precedency Graph\" Test.\n\n-> Procedure:\n    - We will make a graph with Transaction numbers as nodes.\n    - For each operation from first position to last position, identify the conflicting operation.\n    - Make a directed edge from the transaction of the current operation to conflicting operation. \n    - Do this till last.\n    - If the graph contains a cycle, then it is not \"CONFLICT SERIALIZABLE\".\n    - we can also check this by using topological sort. Just simulate KHAN'S ALGORITHM.\n    - And, # conflict equivalent serial schedules that can be formed = # distinct topological orders that can be formed.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Module 7: Transaction Management and Recovery\n\n\nTransaction, Read/Write,Database Consistency\n\n-> A Transaction intuitively is a Single Logical Task. But in the POV of DBMS, it is a unit of program execution that involves sequence of Read, Write and some processing operations on data.\n-> Element or Data-item: Is anything in the Database that can be uniquely named or identified. eg: a record, a relation, a paritcular value of attribute in a record, a block etc.\n-> Database State: Is nothing but DB Snapshot or a DB Instance at a certain point of time.\n-> Consistent Database State is when all the explicit constraints(Schema Constraints) and implicit Constraints(Logical Constraints given by the programmer) are satisfied.\n-> Simplified Read Operation: Read(X) -> Reads the block containing X into the memory buffer of the transaction that initiated the read and stores the value of X onto a variable X on MM.\n-> Simplified Write Operation: Write(X) -> Reads the block containing X into the memory buffer of the transaction that initiated the write and stoers the value of X ONLY IF the write requires \n    the old value of X for updating. And writes the new value to X in the MM and stores the block immediately in the disk.\n\n-> For above simplified reads and writes, we made these assumptions which are not correct for actual reads and writes:\n    1) Each transaction has it's own buffer. In reality all transaction share the same buffer.\n    2) Writes store the data immediately on disk. In reality block is written to the disk after the transaction is commited(either right after or after some time has passed).\n\n-> But these assumptions doesn't make our analysis of transactions incorrect. They produce same results as the actual events.\n-> Transactions are COMMITED after they are completed.\n-> If some error occurs in the middle of transaction at any step, then it is ABORTED OR ROLLEDBACK.\n\n-> Executing multiple transactions concurrently can cause inconsistencies and wrong results to occur.\n\n\nACID Properties:\n\n-> These are some properties that must be satisfied by both programmer and DBMS for correct results.\n-> Atomicity: Treat a sequence of operations as one single unit. If all of them are not completed then rollback previous operations.\n    Basically, treats each transaction as \"All or Nothing\"\n-> Consistency: This is not talking about data base consistency. All acid properties help in achieving DB consistency. \n    This particular property in context of ACID is telling that programmer should write correct transactions for the fact that there is no way\n    to deduce the correctness and consistency by DBMS as this is purely logical and depedent on programmer.\n    So, when we say a DB is consistent, we are assuming that this property is satisfied.\n-> Isolation: Provide an illusion to transactions that it is the only thing executing at a time, even though multiple transactions are executhing concurrently.\n-> Durability: If a transaction is commited. The changes must reflect on the disk no matter what happens.\n\n\nSerializability:\n\n-> Schedule: When transactions are executing concurrently in a interleaved fashion, then the order of execution of\n    operations from multiple transactions is known as a Schedule.\n-> If the operations are not interleaved, then it is called \"SERIAL SCHEDULE\". They always preserve consistency.\n\n-> Providing Isolation to transactions that are running concurrently is a very important task.\n-> To do this, DBMS executes transactions using a serializable schedule, the schedule that gurantess to produce same effects as a serial schedule.\n-> A schedule is \"serializable\" iff it produces same effect on database state as SOME serial schedule does on all possible input db states.\n\n-> However, It is important to note that the state of the DB after all such serial schedules is not same. \n-> That is, the order T1, T3, T4, T2 and T2, T3, T1, T4 may produce different state but the state is guranteed to be consistent.\n\n-> This is the definition of serializability in general.\n-> This definition is very broad in the sense that, to detect if a schedule is serializable, the operations that we perform on the data will play a huge role in determining\n    serializability. Ans since we cannot predict the kind of operations that a query might perform on data, it is very difficult to implement this definition.\n\n-> So, we ignore the \"computations\" on data and only focus on \"read/write\" operations in the schedule. And thus, see if it is \"Conflict Serializable\".\n\n-> Conflict Pairs: We say that two operations I and J in a schedule are said to be in confict iff:\n    from different transactions AND on same data item AND atleast one of them is a write.\n-> Swapping two consecutive non-conflicting operations of different transactions doesn't affect the result of schedule.\n\n-> Conflict Equivalent Schedules: If a Schedule S can be transformed into a schedule S' by a series of swaps of Non-Conflicting Consecutive operations of different\n    transactions, we say that S and S' are CONFLIT EQUIVALENT.\n-> i.e. two schedules are conflict equivalent if they can be turned one into other by a sequence of swaps of adjacent/consecutive non-conflicting operations.\n\n-> It is important to note that, we cannot swap non-consecutive operations coz, it might result in swapping of operations from same transaction.\n\n-> AND FINALLY, A schedule is said to be CONFLICT SERIALIZABLE, if it is conflict equivalent to some serial schedule.\n-> Conflict equivalence is transitive.\n\n-> In a schedule, while determining serializability, ignore COMMIT, it's position doesn't affect anything.\n-> But, if you encounter an ABORT, no matter the position, igonre the whole transaction in which it appears.\n\n\n-> Now, given a schedule, i.e. the order of execution of operations from different transactions, how do you find if it is \"CONFLICT SERIALIZABLE\"?\n-> We use something called \"Precedency Graph\" Test.\n\n-> Procedure:\n    - We will make a graph with Transaction numbers as nodes.\n    - For each operation from first position to last position, identify the conflicting operation.\n    - Make a directed edge from the transaction of the current operation to conflicting operation. \n    - Do this till last.\n    - If the graph contains a cycle, then it is not \"CONFLICT SERIALIZABLE\".\n    - we can also check this by using topological sort. Just simulate KHAN'S ALGORITHM.\n    - And, # conflict equivalent serial schedules that can be formed = # distinct topological orders that can be formed.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
